.Dd November 2, 2026
.Dt INIT 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm init
.Nd reap orphaned processes and maintain the console session and services
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm /sbin/init
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
runs as process 1.
It reaps orphaned children, continues stopped children, and respawns
the console session when it exits.
A pause between attempts prevents a failing login from being retried
continuously.
It never exits; the kernel panics if init dies.
.Pp
Before the first console session it reads optional
.Pa /etc/vconsole.conf
and applies the keyboard settings with kbd's tools.
See
.Xr vconsole.conf 5 .
A failure is reported and does not prevent login.
.Pp
One source produces four builds, selected by the image and installed as
.Pa /sbin/init :
.Bl -tag -width init-recovery
.It Sy init-shell
Starts
.Pa /bin/sh
on the console in test images.
.It Sy init-login
Starts only
.Pa /bin/login .
No shell fallback is compiled into this build.
.It Sy init-metal
Starts the console login and supervises the system log, BusyBox's
.Ic syslogd
run as
.Ql syslogd -n -O /var/log/messages -s 200 -b 1 ,
which binds its socket where
.Pa /dev/log
leads and writes what
.Ic logger
and
.Xr syslog 3
send to
.Pa /var/log/messages ;
see
.Xr busybox-sysklogd 8 .
It supervises the SSH server:
OpenSSH's
.Xr sshd 8
where the image has
.Pa /usr/sbin/sshd ,
as the GNU system's root does, and
.Xr dropbear 8
on every other image.
Where the image has
.Pa /usr/bin/dbus-daemon
and the user
.Ql messagebus ,
as the GNU system's root does, it also supervises D-Bus's system bus,
started as
.Ql dbus-daemon --system --nofork --nopidfile --nosyslog
under that user's ids, with no supplementary groups.
Before each start it makes the bus's directory,
.Pa /var/run/dbus ,
owned by that user, and, when the file is missing,
.Pa /etc/machine-id ,
32 lowercase hexadecimal digits from the kernel's random numbers, which
the bus and its clients read as the machine's identity.
The bus is started as its own user because, started as root, it would
drop to that user with libcap-ng, which keeps a part of root's
capabilities across the change, and this kernel keeps all of a process's
capabilities or none; see
.Xr capget 2 .
An image with the program and no such user reports
.Ql init: dbus: no user messagebus
at boot and runs no bus.
Where the image has
.Pa /usr/sbin/crond ,
as the GNU system's root does, it supervises cronie's cron daemon, run as
.Ql crond -n ,
which logs to the system log and sends it what a job prints, since no
image has a mail transfer agent; see
.Xr cron 8 .
While
.Pa /etc/ntp.conf
has a line
.Ql server Ar NAME ,
as the GNU system's root has, it supervises BusyBox's
.Xr ntpd 1 ,
run as
.Ql ntpd -n ,
which takes the time from those servers and writes each step of the
clock and each error to the service log.
The file is read again whenever ntpd is not running, so a server written
into it is used within a second, and one written while ntpd runs is used
once
.Ql killall ntpd
has ended it.
On a boot with
.Ql rach2.ip=none ,
it runs BusyBox's
.Ql ifup -a
to configure the network; see
.Xr ifup 1 .
Then, where the image has
.Pa /etc/ifplugd/ifplugd.action
and
.Ql eth0
exists, it supervises BusyBox's
.Xr ifplugd 1 ,
run as
.Ql ifplugd -n -s -I -M -p -i eth0 -r /etc/ifplugd/ifplugd.action -m iff -u 0 -d 0 ,
whose action takes the interface down with
.Ic ifdown
when its link goes and up with
.Ic ifup
when it comes, so a cable moved to another network gives that network's
lease at once; and where the image has
.Pa /sbin/ndwatch ,
it supervises
.Xr ndwatch 8
for
.Ql eth0 ,
which follows the routers' DNS servers and starts DHCPv6 when they ask
for it.
.Pp
On a machine with a Wi-Fi radio, while
.Ql wlan0
exists and the image has
.Pa wpa_supplicant
and
.Pa wpa_cli
in
.Pa /bin ,
as the musl systems stage them, or in
.Pa /usr/sbin ,
as the GNU root installs them, and
.Pa /etc/wpa_supplicant/wpa_action
and
.Pa /etc/wpa_supplicant/wlan0.conf ,
whatever the boot line says, it supervises
.Xr wpa_supplicant 8 ,
run as
.Ql wpa_supplicant -W -D nl80211 -i wlan0 -c /etc/wpa_supplicant/wlan0.conf
with a umask of 077, since it writes the networks' passphrases into its
configuration;
.Xr wpa_cli 8 ,
run as
.Ql wpa_cli -i wlan0 -p /var/run/wpa_supplicant -a /etc/wpa_supplicant/wpa_action
once the control socket is there, which
.Fl W
makes wpa_supplicant wait for, so that wpa_cli sees the first connection;
and
.Xr ndwatch 8
for
.Ql wlan0 .
The action starts BusyBox's
.Xr udhcpc 1
on
.Ql wlan0
when the station joins a network and stops it, giving the lease up, when
it leaves one; a roam between the access points of one network keeps the
lease.
ifplugd does not follow
.Ql wlan0 ,
since its action's
.Ic ifdown
would take down the card wpa_supplicant owns.
.Xr wifi 1
adds and removes the networks.
.Pp
The lease script gives each interface its own default route, at metric 100
for a cable and 600 for Wi-Fi, and the resolver's file lists the cable's
servers first, so a machine on both sends by the cable while it is
plugged in, by Wi-Fi once it is pulled, and by the cable again when it
returns.
init gives IPv6 the same order: once each of
.Ql eth0
and
.Ql wlan0
is there, it writes 100 and 600 to its
.Pa /proc/sys/net/ipv6/conf/ Ns Ar NAME Ns Pa /ra_defrtr_metric ,
the metric of the default routes its routers advertise, which
.Xr proc 5
describes.
.It Sy init-recovery
Provides the network and service functions of the metal build
and the recovery duties.
With
.Ql rach2.fsck
on the boot line, it first runs
.Ql e2fsck -p
on each partition
.Pa /dev/disk Ns Ar N Ns Pa p Ns Ar M ,
for each disk
.Ar N
from 0 to 7 and partition
.Ar M
from 1 to 15,
whose superblock has the ext2 magic.
Its console session is a shell without a login, since the image has no
accounts of the machine's; see
.Xr recovery 7 .
.El
.Pp
The builds that start
.Pa /bin/login
on the console give it
.Ev LOGIN_TIMEOUT
set to 0, so the console's prompt waits without a limit; see
.Xr login 1 .
.Pp
On a boot with
.Ql rach2.console=term ,
.Sy init-login
and
.Sy init-metal
start
.Ql /bin/term -l
as the console session in place of
.Pa /bin/login ,
and
.Xr term 1
runs the login inside itself on the whole framebuffer.
Before the first session init opens
.Pa /fb
and asks it for the framebuffer, and gives the screen back at once; on
the VGA's text mode, which has none, the session is
.Pa /bin/login
for the whole boot, with no failed start of term.
Each start of term is said on the console.
A logout, which ends term with status 0, starts term again.
A failure of term, an exec error, a status other than 0, a signal or an
end within two seconds of its start, is reported on the console and
followed at once by
.Pa /bin/login
on the kernel console, which the kernel gives back whole when term ends;
the end of that session starts term again.
Neither path starts a shell without a login.
The sealed disk's menu carries the option, and no test image's does, so
the machine cases keep their login on COM1.
.Pp
Every build clears two levels of
.Pa /var/run
at boot, so that a directory such as the bus's goes with what is in it,
makes
.Pa /var/run/utmp ,
where glibc's programs keep the records of who is logged in, with only
the record of this boot, appends that record to
.Pa /var/log/wtmp ,
and reads
.Pa /etc/rach2/limits .
The boot record is sysvinit's: its type is BOOT_TIME, its user
.Ql reboot ,
its host the kernel's release and its time the
.Li btime
of
.Pa /proc/stat ;
coreutils'
.Xr who 1
.Fl b
and
.Xr uptime 1
take the boot time from it, and util-linux's
.Xr last 1
.Fl x
lists it.
It sets these resource limits on itself before starting any child;
see
.Xr limits 7 .
It starts
.Pa /bin/update
and keeps it running.
.Pp
Every build keeps BusyBox's
.Xr acpid 1
running, as
.Ql acpid -d ,
on an image that has
.Pa /etc/acpid.conf ,
while the kernel has
.Pa /dev/input/event0 ;
acpid opens the event devices there are when it starts.
It writes each action it runs, and what the action prints, to the service
log.
.Pa /etc/acpi.map
names the keys acpid acts on and replaces its built-in map whole, and
.Pa /etc/acpid.conf
names each key's action, a file under
.Pa /etc/acpi
that acpid runs with no argument.
A script staged under several names takes its direction from the name it
is run by.
The images that have a console user stage these actions:
.Bl -tag -width brightness/down
.It Pa power
The power button: runs BusyBox's
.Ic poweroff ,
which asks init to power off.
.It Pa volume/up , Pa volume/down
The volume keys: move the volume by 5 of 100; see
.Xr mixer 1 .
.It Pa volume/mute
The mute key: mutes the volume, or unmutes it when it is muted.
.It Pa brightness/up , Pa brightness/down
The brightness keys: step the level of every backlight of
.Pa /sys/class/backlight
by a tenth of its
.Pa max_brightness .
A step down stops at a twentieth, so that the key never turns the screen
black, and a step up at the maximum; both are at least 1.
The script reads the backlights from the directory
.Ev BACKLIGHT_CLASS
names, when it is set.
.It Pa wifi
The airplane key: when any Wi-Fi switch of
.Pa /sys/class/rfkill
has its soft block clear, blocks every Wi-Fi switch, and otherwise
unblocks every one, with
.Xr rfkill 8 ;
a hard block stays.
.El
.Pp
acpid starts an action for each key without waiting for the one before.
The volume, brightness and wifi actions each hold an exclusive
.Xr flock 2
lock on
.Pa /var/run/acpi.lock ,
or on the file
.Ev ACPI_LOCK
names when it is set and not empty, from their start to their end, so
that they run one at a time and each starts from the state the one before
it left.
.Pp
Every build keeps each backlight's level across a reboot, as
systemd-backlight does on Linux.
At boot it writes the level kept in
.Pa /var/lib/backlight/ Ns Ar NAME
back to the backlight
.Ar NAME ,
kept between a twentieth of its
.Pa max_brightness ,
and at least 1, and that maximum, so that a level saved at 0 does not
start the machine with its screen dark.
When it stops the machine, it saves each backlight's level there, under a
temporary name renamed into place.
It keeps at most four backlights, those present at boot.
.Pp
Every build keeps the mixer's levels across a reboot, as alsactl store and
restore keep them on Linux.
At boot, after it empties
.Pa /var/run
and before it starts acpid or any session, it reads the channels of
.Pa /dev/mixer
from its
.Dv SOUND_MIXER_READ_DEVMASK
and sets each one that
.Pa /var/lib/mixer
names to the level kept there, with
.Dv MIXER_WRITE ;
the file has one line a channel,
.Ql NAME L:R ,
NAME being OSS's name of the channel, such as
.Ql vol .
A missing file leaves the driver's levels alone, and so does a malformed
one, after the diagnostic below; a machine with no mixer keeps nothing.
It puts
.Xr mixer 1 Ns 's
record of a mute back from
.Pa /var/lib/mixer.mute
to
.Pa /var/run/mixer.mute ,
so that a volume muted at the halt is still muted, and can be unmuted,
after it.
When it stops the machine, it saves each channel's level and the record
there, under temporary names renamed into place, or removes the kept record
when there is none.
.Nm
saves only when it stops the machine and
.Xr mixer 1
writes nothing there itself, so a level set and then lost to a power cut,
or to a
.Fl f
that stops the machine without signalling
.Nm ,
is not kept, as on Linux without a running daemon.
.Pp
Every build applies the kernel settings of
.Pa /etc/sysctl.conf ,
where the image has one, with BusyBox's
.Ql sysctl -q -p ,
before the stable secret below, so a card restarted for the secret makes
its addresses under them; a line sysctl cannot apply is reported and the
rest applied.
The installed systems set
.Va use_tempaddr
to 2 there, for
.Ql all
and
.Ql default ,
so an outgoing IPv6 connection takes a temporary address while the stable
one still answers; see
.Xr ipv6 7 .
.Pp
Every build keeps IPv6's stable secret across a reboot, so that the
addresses a router's prefix gives the machine stay the same from one boot
to the next, as Linux's do under systemd.
At boot, before the first session, it writes the secret kept in
.Pa /var/lib/ipv6-stable-secret
to
.Pa /proc/sys/net/ipv6/conf/default/stable_secret ,
which
.Xr proc 5
describes, making the file from 16 random bytes when it is missing or
does not hold an address, and turns IPv6 off and on again on each card
that already took an address from a router's prefix, which makes it
again with the secret.
.Pp
Services receive
.Pa /dev/null
as standard input.
The service log is
.Pa /var/log/services .
When the log is available, init directs service standard output and
standard error there.
During operation, it empties the log in place when it has reached
256 KiB and writes:
.Dl init: the service log reached its limit and was emptied
.Pp
A service child exits before execution if a required descriptor cannot be
opened or duplicated.
Network setup runs in a child with the same redirection, leaving init's
console descriptors available for later login sessions.
The network worker and the SSH server's child reset SIGUSR1, SIGUSR2, and
SIGTERM to their default dispositions while they are blocked across fork,
then restore the inherited signal mask.
The bus's child does the same.
These signals terminate any of these children, the SSH server's while it
waits for
.Ql ssh-keygen -A
too, instead of shutting down the machine.
The console is reserved for the console session.
The kernel stops writing to the screen when init starts;
see
.Xr bootlog 4 .
Dropbear is started with
.Fl g
and
.Xr sshd 8
with
.Ql PermitRootLogin prohibit-password
unless
.Xr sshd.conf 5
permits root's password, when
.Fl g
is left out and
.Xr sshd 8
is given
.Ql PermitRootLogin yes .
.Xr sshd 8
runs in the foreground with
.Fl D
and writes its log to the service log with
.Fl e .
Before each of its starts,
.Ql ssh-keygen -A
makes the host keys missing from
.Pa /etc/ssh .
Each start of either server is reported on the console as
.Ql init: sshd: started as pid N .
.Pp
.Nm
handles the signals sent by the BusyBox shutdown applets, each by saving
the backlights' and the mixer's levels and stopping the machine:
.Bl -tag -width SIGUSR1
.It Dv SIGUSR1
Halt the machine.
.It Dv SIGUSR2
Power off the machine.
.It Dv SIGTERM
Restart the machine.
.El
.Pp
The three are blocked while one is handled, so a second request, such as
the second
.Ic poweroff
of a power button the firmware reports twice for one press, waits and is
never answered.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /etc/rach2/sshd.conf
.It Pa /etc/acpi.map , Pa /etc/acpid.conf
acpid's map and actions, whose presence starts it.
.It Pa /etc/acpi
The actions acpid runs.
.It Pa /var/run/acpi.lock
The lock the volume and brightness actions hold while they run.
.It Pa /var/lib/backlight
Each backlight's level, saved when the machine stops and written back at
boot.
.It Pa /var/lib/ipv6-stable-secret
IPv6's stable secret, an address's text, mode 0600, made at the first
boot.
.It Pa /var/lib/mixer , Pa /var/lib/mixer.mute
The mixer's levels and its record of a mute, saved when the machine stops
and written back at boot.
.It Pa /etc/rach2/limits
Initial resource limits.
.It Pa /etc/sysctl.conf
Kernel settings applied at boot.
.It Pa /etc/ifplugd/ifplugd.action
The action of
.Xr ifplugd 1 ,
whose presence starts it on a boot that hands the network to userland.
.It Pa /etc/wpa_supplicant/wlan0.conf
wpa_supplicant's configuration and the known Wi-Fi networks, readable only
by root.
.It Pa /etc/wpa_supplicant/wpa_action
The action of
.Xr wpa_cli 8 ,
which starts and stops the DHCP client on
.Ql wlan0 .
.It Pa /etc/rach2/sshd.conf
SSH server policy.
.It Pa /etc/ssh
OpenSSH's configuration and host keys, on the GNU system's root.
.It Pa /usr/sbin/sshd
OpenSSH's server, which the metal build runs in place of Dropbear
when the image has it.
.It Pa /var/log/services
Service output and diagnostics, with the SSH server's record of each
login, readable only by root.
.It Pa /etc/machine-id
The machine's identity, made at the first boot of the GNU system's root.
.It Pa /usr/bin/dbus-daemon
D-Bus's system bus, which the metal build runs where the image has it.
.It Pa /usr/sbin/crond
The cron daemon, which the metal build runs where the image has it.
.It Pa /etc/ntp.conf
The NTP servers, by which the metal build runs
.Xr ntpd 1
while the file names one.
.It Pa /var/run
Entries cleared at boot, with the entries of its directories, which are
then removed; a directory deeper than that is reported if it is not empty.
.It Pa /var/run/dbus
The bus's directory, with its socket,
.Pa system_bus_socket .
.It Pa /var/run/utmp
The login records, the boot's first: OpenSSH's server on the GNU system's
root adds a session's record, which
.Xr who 1
reads; the musl programs keep none.
.It Pa /var/log/wtmp , Pa /var/log/btmp
The history of logins and of failed ones, which OpenSSH's server adds to
and util-linux's
.Xr last 1
and
.Xr lastb 1
read on the GNU system's root.
init makes each at boot when it is missing, the first with mode 0664 and
the second with 0600, and keeps what they have, adding the boot's record
to the first.
.El
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
A kept mixer file that is not lines of
.Ql NAME L:R
leaves the levels alone, with:
.Dl init: mixer: /var/lib/mixer: malformed; levels left alone
.Pp
The recovery file system check reports its status as:
.Dl init: e2fsck: /dev/diskNpM: status S
.Pp
On a boot with
.Ql rach2.console=term ,
each start of term, and each failure, with what failed, are reported as:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
init: console: starting term
init: cannot exec /bin/term
init: console: term: not run; login on the kernel console
init: console: term: status N; login on the kernel console
init: console: term: killed by signal N; login on the kernel console
init: console: term: ended at its start; login on the kernel console
init: console: /fb: ERROR; login on the kernel console
.Ed
.Pp
A secret that cannot be kept or put in force is reported as:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
init: ipv6: cannot keep IPv6's stable secret in /var/lib/ipv6-stable-secret, so it is this boot's: ERROR
init: ipv6: cannot set IPv6's stable secret: ERROR
init: ipv6: CARD: cannot restart IPv6 with the stable secret: ERROR
.Ed
